Final verification report: US-5.3 publish order.reserved once an order's stock is fully reserved

Automated tests: sbt test (full suite)
Result: 111 passed, 0 failed, 92.20% statement / 93.00% branch coverage.
Includes two Testcontainers-Kafka tests specific to this track's end-to-end
behavior: a synthetic inventory.stock-reserved event against a matching
Pending order results in both the status becoming Reserved AND an
order.reserved event published with the correct orderId/customerId/
totalCents; a synthetic inventory.stock-reservation-failed event never
publishes order.reserved.

Manual verification (prompt-off: via committed script, not an interactive walkthrough):
Command: ./scripts/verify-order-reserved-end-to-end.sh
Verifies what's reliably provable live: order-service boots cleanly with
OrderEventPublisher newly wired into Main.scala's startup chain (a new
required resource, same risk category as US-8.1's Redis config addition),
and the synchronous checkout path is unaffected.

Scope reduction and the reason for it: this script originally attempted the
full live round trip against a real running order-service + real Postgres +
real Kafka (docker-compose, not Testcontainers) - checkout -> sync reserve
(stubbed) -> synthetic inventory.stock-reserved -> order flips to Reserved ->
order.reserved published. That round trip reliably (4/4 attempts) hit a
local-only broker race: order-service's new OrderEventPublisher producer
connects at the same moment StockEventConsumer's merged stream joins its
consumer groups, and the stock-reserved consumer's first poll then crashes
with `java.lang.NullPointerException: Cannot read the array length because
"bytes" is null` in fs2-kafka's String deserializer (Deserializer.scala:203).
The reservation-failed consumer, which has no concurrent producer connecting
at the same moment, was unaffected every time - strong evidence this is
specific to the new producer+consumer startup timing on this exact
single-node KRaft docker-compose broker, not a logic bug in this track's
code. Not reproducible via Testcontainers' Kafka (used by
StockEventConsumerSuite, which proves this exact round trip, including
asserting the published payload's exact fields, and passes cleanly every
run). Mitigation attempts tried and exhausted before descoping, per the
user's explicit "just proceed, we have a note already": Kafka-readiness wait
before starting order-service, extra stabilization sleep, pre-seeding both
topics with a throwaway message, offset-based (not consumer-group-based)
seed confirmation. None eliminated the race. Descoped to the boot+checkout
check above; the round trip itself is authoritatively proven by the
Testcontainers suite.

This is a genuine, narrow finding worth remembering if this local Kafka
setup is ever used for other live multi-consumer+producer verification
scripts in this repo: starting a producer and a consumer against the same
fresh single-node KRaft broker at the same moment is the trigger, not the
consumer alone (Phase 2's verify-order-event-publisher.sh, which starts no
competing consumer, never hit it; Phase 1's reserve-resilience and
stock-event-consumer tracks' own scripts, which start no competing
producer, never hit it either).
